          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Many Watts Does a Gas Furnace Use?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the blower, because it decides almost everything. Older and builder-grade furnaces use a PSC (permanent split capacitor) blower motor, typically 1/3 to 3/4 horsepower. Those commonly draw 400 to 900 watts running, and the nameplate amps on the motor are the best guide: a 1/2 HP PSC blower rated around 6 to 7 amps at 120 volts is drawing roughly 700 to 800 watts. PSC motors also pull a brief starting surge of two to three times their running draw.
          </p>

          <p>
            Newer and higher-efficiency furnaces often use an ECM (electronically commutated) variable-speed blower. These are dramatically more efficient at the lower speeds they spend most of their time at, commonly 100 to 300 watts in heating mode, and they ramp up gently rather than slamming on, so the starting surge is small. At full cooling speed an ECM can still pull 500 watts or more, but that matters for air conditioning, not for an outage in January.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other components are smaller but not trivial. A draft inducer motor typically uses 50 to 250 watts and runs whenever the burner is lit. The hot surface igniter on many furnaces is rated somewhere around 300 to 450 watts, though some silicon-nitride igniters draw less, and it only glows for roughly 15 to 60 seconds per cycle. The control board, gas valve and transformer use a few tens of watts. Add it up and a PSC furnace runs around 600 to 900 watts while firing, and an ECM furnace around 250 to 500.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sequence matters for inverter sizing. On a call for heat, the inducer starts, then the igniter glows, then gas opens and lights, then after a delay the blower comes on. The igniter usually switches off before the blower starts, so the biggest momentary load is the PSC blower's starting surge on top of the inducer, not everything at once. Look at the furnace's data plate, which lists total amps, and multiply by 120 to get a worst-case running figure.
          </p>

