        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Runtime Math: How Long Will a Battery Run a Furnace?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use this formula: hours of runtime equal battery capacity in watt-hours, times about 0.85 for inverter losses and reserve, divided by the furnace's average draw. The trick is that average draw is not the running draw. A furnace cycles on and off with the thermostat, so its average depends on how cold it is outside, how well the house holds heat and where you set the thermostat.
          </p>

          <p>
            A furnace that is correctly sized for its house typically runs 30 to 50 percent of the time on a cold winter day, and can approach continuous running in a severe cold snap. If a PSC furnace draws 700 watts while firing and runs 40 percent of the time, its average is about 280 watts plus a few watts of standby. An ECM furnace drawing 350 watts at the same duty cycle averages about 140 watts. That gap is the single biggest factor in runtime.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the numbers for a 2,000Wh station. With 1,700 usable watt-hours and a 280-watt average, the PSC furnace runs about six hours. The ECM furnace at 140 watts runs about twelve. A large 3,600Wh unit roughly doubles those figures, and expansion batteries extend them further. Our <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">power station sizing guide</a> uses the same method for a whole outage load list.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can stretch runtime without freezing. Lower the thermostat to 62 or 64 degrees, which shortens burner cycles. Close doors and vents to rooms you are not using. Leave the blower fan on Auto, never On, because a PSC blower running continuously can use more energy than the burner cycles themselves. And keep other loads off the station while the furnace is on it, or at least account for them in the math.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recharging is what turns a one-night solution into a multi-day one. Solar panels in winter produce far less than their rating, often a fraction of it on short, overcast days, so plan on solar as a top-up rather than a full refill. Many owners pair a station with a small inverter generator that recharges it for a couple of hours each day; see our comparison of an <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">inverter generator versus a regular generator</a> if that is the plan.
          </p>

