        <div id="hookup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Connect a Hardwired Furnace to a Power Station
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most gas furnaces are wired directly to a dedicated 15 or 20-amp circuit, with a service switch on or near the cabinet that looks like a light switch. There is no plug, so you cannot simply run an extension cord to it. What you must never do is make a so-called suicide cord, a male-to-male cord that backfeeds a wall outlet. It energises exposed prongs, can backfeed the utility line and injure lineworkers, and it bypasses every protection in the panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first legitimate option is a furnace cord conversion. An electrician replaces the hardwired connection with a short appliance cord and installs a receptacle next to the furnace, so in an outage you unplug the furnace from the wall and plug it into the power station's extension cord. Whether this is permitted depends on your local code and the furnace manufacturer's instructions; many jurisdictions allow it, some do not, so ask the electrician before assuming.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second option is a transfer switch. A single-circuit furnace transfer switch installed at the furnace lets you flip between utility power and a power inlet, and is one of the cheapest transfer switch jobs an electrician does. A panel-mounted manual transfer switch or interlock kit goes further, letting the power station feed several essential circuits at once. Our guide to <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">transfer switch types, cost and installation</a> covers the options and typical prices.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third option is a power station built for it. Several large home-backup units have a dedicated input to a matching home panel or transfer switch from the same brand, or a 30-amp twist-lock output designed to feed a manual transfer switch through an inlet box. These cost more, but the wiring is designed for the job. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best portable power station for home backup</a> list notes which models connect to a transfer switch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever route you choose, have a licensed electrician do the installation. The circuit needs to stay protected by its breaker, the neutral and ground have to be handled correctly, and the furnace has to be isolated from utility power while the station feeds it. Those details are also exactly what keeps the furnace from locking out, which is the next section.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEVER DO THIS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No male-to-male cords, ever.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Backfeeding an outlet with a double-ended cord can electrocute you or a utility lineworker and can start a fire. A furnace cord conversion or a transfer switch is the safe way to put battery power on a hardwired furnace.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="grounding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Polarity, Grounding and Flame-Sensor Lockouts
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where most failed furnace-on-battery attempts actually fail. A gas furnace proves its flame using flame rectification: the control board puts a small AC voltage on the flame sensor rod, and the flame conducts a tiny DC current, measured in microamps, to the grounded burner. If the board does not see that current within a few seconds of the gas valve opening, it shuts the gas off, retries, and after a few attempts locks out. The furnace lights, runs for a few seconds and goes out.
          </p>

          <p>
            That circuit needs two things to be right: hot and neutral in the correct positions, and neutral bonded to ground somewhere upstream. In a house on utility power, neutral and ground are bonded at the main panel. Many portable power stations, and many inverter generators, have a floating neutral, meaning neutral is not bonded to ground inside the unit. Plug a furnace directly into a floating-neutral source and the board may report reversed polarity, or see no reliable flame signal and lock out.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the station feeds the house through a transfer switch that keeps the neutral connected to the main panel, the panel's existing bond normally solves this. When the furnace is plugged into the station directly through a cord conversion, the fix owners and electricians commonly use is a neutral-ground bonding plug in a spare outlet on the station, or a station whose manual states a bonded neutral. Polarity also gets reversed by miswired extension cords and adapters, so use a plain three-prong cord and check with a plug-in outlet tester.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not treat bonding as a one-size answer. A bonding plug is for when the furnace is the only thing connected; if the station is also feeding a panel that is already bonded, adding another bond creates a parallel neutral path, which is a safety problem. This is why the electrician who installs the hookup should also decide how the neutral is handled. Our guide on whether <a href="/do-i-need-to-ground-a-portable-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">you need to ground a portable generator</a> explains bonded and floating neutrals in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the furnace keeps locking out on battery but runs fine on utility power, the error code on the control board will usually tell you why. Many boards flash a distinct code for reversed polarity or flame-sense failure. Check polarity first, then the neutral-ground bond, and only then suspect a dirty flame sensor, which is the common cause of the same symptom on grid power.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inverter-specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Inverter Specs That Matter for a Furnace
          </h2>

          <p>
            The inverter must produce a pure sine wave. Every major-brand portable power station does, but cheap standalone inverters and some budget units use a modified sine wave, which can make PSC motors run hot and buzz, confuse ECM motor electronics and disrupt the control board's flame sensing. Our guide to <a href="/pure-sine-wave-vs-modified-sine-wave/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pure sine wave versus modified sine wave</a> explains why motors and electronic controls react badly to the stepped waveform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Continuous output needs to cover the furnace's running draw plus anything else you will run at the same time, with margin. A 1,000-watt inverter technically runs most furnaces, but a 1,800 to 2,400-watt inverter leaves room for the refrigerator and a few lights alongside it. Surge rating matters for PSC blowers: a 700-watt blower that briefly pulls 1,800 to 2,100 watts on start can trip a small inverter's overload protection, which then shuts the furnace down mid-cycle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two features help for overnight use. A UPS or pass-through mode lets the station sit between the wall and the furnace so it switches over automatically when the grid drops, though switchover times vary from under 30 milliseconds to much longer and not every furnace board rides through a slow transfer. And an eco or auto-off setting must be disabled, because a station that shuts its AC output off when it senses a low load will cut power between heating cycles, when the furnace is drawing only a few watts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery chemistry also matters in a furnace closet or cold garage. LiFePO4 batteries generally cannot be charged below freezing, and some stations restrict charging or output in the cold. Keep the station indoors at room temperature, which is fine for a battery unit, and run the furnace's cord to it, rather than parking it in an unheated garage. Unlike a generator, a power station produces no exhaust, so indoor placement is safe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Oil Furnaces, Boilers and Heat Pumps
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oil furnaces work on the same principle as gas furnaces, with a different burner. The oil burner motor drives the fuel pump and fan, typically a 1/7 to 1/5 horsepower motor drawing roughly 150 to 300 watts, and an ignition transformer or electronic igniter adds a modest load. With the blower on top, an oil furnace usually lands in the same 500 to 900-watt range as a PSC gas furnace, with a noticeable starting surge from the burner motor and blower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hot water boilers are often the easiest heating system of all to back up. A gas boiler's controls and inducer use little power, and the circulator pumps that move water to the radiators commonly draw about 40 to 100 watts each for older fixed-speed pumps, and far less for modern ECM circulators. A boiler with one or two zones can run on a mid-size power station for a day or more. The same polarity and grounding sensitivity applies to boiler controls, so treat the hookup the same way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat pumps and electric furnaces are a different story. An electric furnace or heat strip makes heat from electricity at 5,000 to 20,000 watts, far beyond any portable station. A heat pump is much more efficient, but a central unit commonly draws 2,000 to 5,000 watts plus a large compressor surge, and backup heat strips can switch on in cold weather. Only the largest whole-home battery systems handle that. If your house heats electrically, our page on whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-space-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">a power station can run a space heater</a> covers the realistic alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a gas or oil furnace, the shopping list is short: a pure sine wave station of at least 2,000Wh with an 1,800-watt or larger inverter, a safe hookup installed by an electrician, a correct neutral-ground arrangement, and a way to recharge. Get those four right and a battery can keep a house heated through an overnight outage silently, indoors and without fuel to store.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a male-to-male cord to backfeed an outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is the most dangerous shortcut in home backup. A double-ended cord leaves live prongs exposed, can backfeed the utility line and injure lineworkers, and bypasses breaker protection. If the furnace is hardwired, pay an electrician for a cord conversion or a transfer switch. The cost is modest compared with the risk.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the floating neutral</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many power stations have a floating neutral. Plugged straight into a furnace, the control board may flag reversed polarity or fail to sense flame and lock out after three tries. Feed it through a properly bonded panel, or have an electrician confirm whether a neutral-ground bonding plug is appropriate for your setup.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the blower fan set to On</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thermostat fan set to On runs the blower continuously, even when the burner is off. On a PSC furnace that can add several hundred watts around the clock and cut runtime by half or more. Set the fan to Auto during an outage so the blower only runs while the furnace is actually heating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by the furnace's running watts alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The inverter also has to handle the PSC blower's starting surge and anything else plugged in at the same time. A small 1,000-watt unit may run the furnace until the fridge compressor starts with it, then trip. Size the inverter at 1,800 watts or more if other appliances will share the station.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving auto-off or eco mode enabled</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many stations switch off AC output when the load falls below a threshold to save energy. Between heating cycles a furnace draws only a few watts, so the station can shut off and the furnace never restarts. Disable AC auto-off before relying on the station overnight, and check it after firmware updates.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station run a gas furnace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, most residential gas furnaces run on a pure sine wave power station, because electricity only powers the blower, inducer, igniter and controls. Typical draw is 300 to 800 watts while firing. The real challenges are connecting a hardwired furnace safely and making sure the control board sees correct polarity and a neutral-ground bond.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size power station do I need for a gas furnace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For an overnight outage, plan on at least 2,000Wh with an inverter rated 1,800 watts or more. That typically runs an ECM furnace around twelve hours and a PSC furnace around six in cold weather. Larger units of 3,600Wh or more, or expansion batteries, extend that to a full day or longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts does a furnace blower use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A PSC blower motor typically draws 400 to 900 watts running, with a brief start surge of two to three times that. A variable-speed ECM blower usually draws 100 to 300 watts at heating speeds and starts gently. The motor's nameplate amps multiplied by 120 gives a reasonable running estimate for your furnace.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my furnace light and then shut off on battery power?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most often it is a flame-sensing failure caused by a floating neutral or reversed polarity. The control board needs a bonded neutral and correct polarity to detect the flame current, and if it does not see it, it closes the gas valve and eventually locks out. Check the board's flashing error code, polarity and neutral bonding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a battery backup for a gas furnace safe indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A battery power station produces no exhaust, so it is safe indoors when used as the manufacturer directs, unlike any fuel-burning generator. Keep it on a hard, dry surface with clear ventilation around its fans, away from the furnace's combustion air openings and flue, and use a proper hookup rather than improvised cords or adapters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug my furnace into a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it has a plug. Most furnaces are hardwired, so an electrician has to install a cord and receptacle where local code allows, or a transfer switch. Once there is a proper connection, the furnace plugs into the station with a heavy-gauge three-prong extension cord sized for the load.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a power station run an oil furnace or boiler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes. An oil furnace uses roughly the same power as a gas furnace with a PSC blower, typically 500 to 900 watts while firing. Hot water boilers are often easier, since circulator pumps commonly draw 40 to 100 watts or less. Both need pure sine wave power and the same care with polarity and grounding.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

