        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A portable power station is a genuinely good way to keep a gas furnace running through an outage. The furnace only needs a few hundred watts, a 2,000Wh unit covers a cold night for many homes, and an ECM blower roughly doubles the runtime of an older PSC furnace.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Spend your effort on the connection, not the capacity. Have an electrician install a cord conversion or transfer switch, get the neutral-ground bond right so the flame sensor works, disable AC auto-off, and set the fan to Auto. Do that and the furnace will run on battery as reliably as it does on the grid.
          </p>
        </div>

